A16_SMSS_72_M016936_004                                                       
                                                                              
Apollo 16 Soil Mechanics                                                      
Lunar Self-Recording Penetrometer (LSRP) Page A-1                             
                                                                              
Plot of Penetration vs. Stress, Index No. 5 (first test)                      
                                                                              
The hand-drawn plot shows the stress applied at the penetrometer tip as       
a function of depth of penetration as recorded on the lunar drum.             
The plot was created using data from the Data Reduction Table                 
A16_SMSS_72_M016936_017, page B-3.                                            
                                                                              
All 11 Apollo 16 Lunar Self-Recording Penetrometer (LSRP) runs were           
performed by lunar module pilot Charles Duke on the second EVA                
(extravehicular activity) on 22 April 1972. The first four tests were         
done at station 4, which was high in the side of Stone Mountain, in the       
Cinco crater area, at the astronaut-named, roughly 10 meter diameter          
Bench Crater, about 4 km south of the landing site.  (The landing site        
was at 8.9730 S latitude, 15.5002 E longitude.) This was the first stop       
of the second EVA.  The runs at station 4 started with the LSRP index         
set to 5.  All runs were done using the flight unit S/N 2004, lunar drum      
S/N 2008.                                                                     
                                                                              
This run (first run, index 5) was done uphill, south of the rover on the      
top part of the crater. It was performed at 18:24:05 UT (144:30:05            
Mission Elapsed Time, or MET) using the 3.22 square centimeter (0.5           
square inch) cone.  On the plot, stress is given on the abscissa marked       
from 0 to 70 Newtons per square centimeter and 0 to 15 ksf (1 ksf = 1000      
pounds per square foot = 4.788 Newtons per centimeter squared) and            
penetration on the ordinate from 0 to 20 centimeters. All quantities are      
given on a linear scale.                                                      
